<script server>
// Auth-aware nav, decided on the SERVER.
//
// This was a client signal mirroring the session store, and the auth links were
// `:show` bindings on it. stx stamps `x-cloak` on every `:show` and `@if`
// element — correct, because a condition only the client can evaluate would
// otherwise flash the wrong branch — and the cloak rule is
// `[x-cloak]{display:none !important}`. So "Log in", "Sign up" and "Dashboard"
// were display:none until the runtime booted, on every marketing page. Measured
// on the served HTML: 9 cloaked elements on /, 10 on /pricing, 9 on /compare and
// /use-cases. A crawler saw none of them.
//
// The condition was never client-only. The session token is a cookie, and the
// serve path hands this block the request's cookies, so the branch resolves at
// render time and ships as plain markup with no cloak and no flash in either
// direction. Verified: a partial's server block does receive `cookies` and it
// tracks the request.
//
// This does make the response vary by cookie. That costs nothing here — these
// pages already answer `Cache-Control: no-store` and already mint a per-request
// CSRF cookie, so the HTML was never cacheable.
//
// Do NOT reintroduce a client mirror to "keep it reactive". Nothing on a
// marketing page changes auth state in place: signing in navigates, and
// signOut() does a declared full load, so the next render is server-truth
// anyway.
const navAuthed = !!cookies.loghq_token
</script>

{{-- Do NOT write a template comment's opening marker as prose anywhere in this
    file, not even inside a script comment and not even in backticks. The
    renderer scans for it textually: an unpaired opening marker once opened a
    comment that ran to end of file, swallowed the closing script tag and the
    entire nav, and every marketing page rendered as a bare footer. The dev
    server says so plainly — it reports one script opened and none closed —
    which is the fastest way to spot it. Describe the syntax; never quote it. --}}

